id: pm-pae3wo
title: "Cross-version workspace compatibility matrix: prove that workspaces written by older and newer pm releases stay readable and mergeable"
description: "Build a cross-version compatibility matrix that exercises old and new readers, writers, migrations, and merge drivers so mixed-version agent fleets cannot silently corrupt or lose workspace state."
type: Task
status: open
priority: 1
tags[7]: ci,compatibility,format-version,migration,multi-agent,quality-gate,storage
created_at: "2026-07-24T23:47:05.940Z"
updated_at: "2026-07-31T10:16:08.267Z"
author: maintainer-agent
estimated_minutes: 1500
acceptance_criteria: "The matrix covers at least the previous two release lines writing and the current build reading, plus the reverse direction; Forward-incompatible reads fail loudly with an actionable error rather than silently misparsing or dropping fields; Backward-compatible reads preserve unknown fields on round-trip so an older reader cannot destroy a newer writer's data; Merge drivers are exercised across version skew, not only within one version; The declared compatibility window is documented and the matrix fails when the code drifts from the declaration"
goal: ci-detects-every-defect
objective: A workspace is never bricked or silently misread by a version skew between agents
value: "Thousands of agents on many machines will not run the same pm build. Format skew between writers and readers is therefore the normal case, not an edge case, and nothing currently tests it."
impact: Protects the durability promise across upgrades and makes the storage format_version policy in the GA contract enforceable rather than declarative.
outcome: "A CI matrix writes workspaces with previous releases, reads and mutates them with the current build, and asserts defined behaviour in both directions including refusal where refusal is correct."
why_now: "The format_version field exists and migrations are planned, but there is no test proving what happens when a newer writer meets an older reader — the exact situation a staggered agent fleet creates daily."
parent: pm-o2kc
risk: medium
confidence: medium
